epistle (0.12.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Security: the Bayesian filter no longer lets one account decide what
    another receives. A message with more than one local recipient, or
    addressed to an alias or a list, used to be scored by the corpus of the
    first local account of the envelope (the first member of an alias), and
    that verdict (Junk, or a `550` at DATA) applied to every recipient, so an
    account that taught its own corpus a spammer's text as ham could let that
    spam through to anyone addressed alongside it. An account's own corpus now
    scores a message only when that account is the single local recipient,
    was addressed as itself and has at least 200 ham and 200 spam of its own.
    Everything else is scored by the shared corpus when that has as much,
    which changes delivery: mail to aliases, lists and accounts that have not
    trained their own corpus is now subject to `bayes_junk_at` and
    `bayes_reject_at` through the shared corpus, where before it was scored
    only when the first member had a trained corpus of its own.
  * Security: training the Bayesian corpus on a message is bounded. The shared
    corpus learns from every message the server accepts or rejects, whoever
    sent it, and it tokenized the whole message (up to 25 MiB, a few million
    words) and wrote one row per word, one statement each, holding a lock
    that the lessons of the accounts waited for. A message now teaches the
    corpus from at most 5000 distinct words of the first and last 128 KiB of
    what it says, in one statement; when it has more, the first and last 1000
    are kept and the rest chosen by a keyed hash of the word, so where the
    sender puts the others decides nothing. The transactions of a training and
    of `epistle account remove` now take their locks in one order, which ends
    the deadlocks between them. The shared corpus is not pruned: a message of
    5000 new words adds about 1.7 MB of table and 3.5 MB of log, and one
    address may teach 30 such messages an hour.
  * Security: scoring a message with the Bayesian corpus is bounded the same
    way. It tokenized, hashed and looked up every word of a message of up to
    25 MiB inside the session of an unauthenticated sender (338 MiB of heap
    for one message). The filter now reads what a person reads: the headers
    and the text parts, decoded, not attachments or images, and whitespace and
    one-letter words cost nothing; a message that two readers could split
    into parts differently is read as raw bytes as well. A message under 128 KiB + 128 KiB of
    words and 5000 distinct words scores on all of it, as before; one with an
    attachment now scores as the message without it (before, any attachment of
    more than about 125 KiB gave the message a score of 0.5). A message over
    either bound is scored on part of it and never below 0.5, which is not
    Junk with the default `bayes_junk_at` of 0.95 and only reaches a second
    opinion with `[antispam_llm]`: spam padded with more than 256 KiB of words,
    or with more than 5000 distinct words, with the spam in the middle, can
    escape the filter. That is the detection lost to the bound.
  * Security: the shared Bayesian corpus no longer starts a task per message,
    and a sender cannot decide it alone. The server queues what it learns
    (64 jobs, 2 workers) instead of spawning a task for each message. Ham and
    spam are budgeted apart: a client address and the wider block around it
    (IPv4 /32 and /24, IPv6 /64 and /48) may add 30 and 120 jobs per hour,
    a sender domain 30 but only when the server verified it (the envelope
    sender is written by the client, so a forged domain spends nothing of the
    real one), a whole network (IPv4 /16, IPv6 /32) 125 and 16 in flight,
    and the whole server 2000, the last quarter of them kept for networks that
    have used few. The tables of keys drop the least recently used entry when
    full instead of refusing new senders. A job over its budget or that finds
    the queue full is dropped, without spending any budget when it was the
    queue that had no room, and counted in `mail_bayes_training_dropped_total`.
    The server also learns ham only from a sender whose SPF or DKIM passed,
    so an unverified sender cannot teach the corpus that its text is ham;
    bounces, SubjectPass resends and mail from a sender with neither teach
    nothing. A sender that fails the checks can still have text of its
    choosing learned as spam within its budget. Behind a container network
    that hides the client address (rootless Podman with a bridge) the server
    sees one address and learns at the rate of one.
  * Security: the Bayesian filter reads mail the way a client shows it, in
    more cases. Attachments no longer use up the budget for decoding text, so
    text sent as base64 or quoted-printable after a large file is read; a part
    that declares two transfer encodings is read under each; text in UTF-16 or
    an 8-bit charset, and RFC 2047 encoded words in the headers, are read as a
    person sees them; zero width characters and soft hyphens no longer split a
    word; and a message the filter could not follow (too deep, too many parts,
    a text part or a message inside it past the 2 MiB decoding budget) counts
    as not read in full, so it scores at least 0.5. Letters outside ASCII are
    now lowercased as well: a token with a capital outside ASCII ("Привет",
    "École") that the corpus learned before this release is not found under
    its new lower case form and is learned again; the old rows stay in the
    table, unused, because the corpus keeps keyed hashes and cannot convert
    them. Tokens in ASCII are unchanged. A message that cannot be reduced at all
    teaches nothing and spends none of the sender's budget.
  * JMAP: Sieve scripts (RFC 9661). The session advertises
    `urn:ietf:params:jmap:sieve`, and `SieveScript/get`, `/query`, `/set` and
    `/validate` manage the same scripts ManageSieve does: a script written
    over either is listed, valid and active in the other, with an id that
    survives a rename. Exactly one script is active, switched with
    `onSuccessActivateScript` and `onSuccessDeactivateScript`. The limits
    apply to both protocols: 1 MiB per script, 50 scripts, names of 128
    characters and 200 octets, and a `require` of an extension the
    interpreter does not run is refused (ManageSieve answers
    `NO (QUOTA/MAXSIZE)` and `NO (QUOTA/MAXSCRIPTS)`, and HAVESPACE checks
    them). A refusal names the line and never repeats the script. See
    docs/configuration.md.
  * Sieve: a script is now bounded in what one run may spend. `:matches` no
    longer overflows the stack or takes exponential time on a pattern of many
    wildcards (a 100 KB script aborted the server), and a text of any length
    still matches; `set "a" "${a}${a}"` repeated no longer builds gigabytes
    (variables are cut to 4096 octets, 64 KiB per run); a run is stopped past
    20000 steps or 256 MiB of work (what it decided about where the
    message goes stands, what it would have sent is dropped); `fileinto` files
    into a folder once and into at most 8 folders; no chain of `not` nests past
    64 levels (about 200000 of them overflowed the stack). A filter that files
    by a header (`fileinto "${1}"`) makes no folder past 200 in the account,
    counting every level, and keeps the message in INBOX instead.
  * Sieve `redirect`: at most 4 addresses per message and an address once, and
    the target must be a valid address (a line break in it was written into
    the outbound `RCPT TO` command). A redirect to something that is not an
    address keeps the message instead of dropping it behind a redirect that
    never happened. Upgrading: an active script that redirects to more than 4
    different addresses now forwards to the first 4 and the log says how many
    it ignored; a script that relied on `redirect` of a malformed address now
    leaves the message in INBOX.
  * Sieve scripts (ManageSieve and JMAP, one check): a `require` of an extension
    the interpreter does not run is refused when saving, and the list now
    includes `subaddress`, `comparator-i;octet` and `comparator-i;ascii-casemap`.
    Upgrading: a stored script that requires an unsupported extension keeps
    running but cannot be saved again until it is fixed; names with U+2028 or
    U+2029, with white space at either end, with a bidirectional control or not
    in normal form C are no longer listed (the log names the account once;
    rename the files in `<data_dir>/accounts/<account>/sieve/`); an account
    with more than 50 scripts keeps them all and cannot add one until it is
    under the limit. The active marker is no longer trimmed.
  * Sieve: what a test reads of a message is a fixed window: the first 1 MiB
    of the body, and per header name of a lookup the first 1000 values and
    64 KiB (16 names at most), so no header hides the others listed with it.
    Upgrading: a stored script with a `:matches` key over 256 characters or 32
    wildcards no longer matches (the test is false, the rest of the script
    runs, and the log names the account once an hour); it used to match. A
    message bigger than that no longer makes a script run out of work, and a rule that matches text past the
    window does not see it: the log says so once an hour per account and the
    new counter `mail_sieve_inspection_truncated_total` (alert metric
    `sieve_inspection_truncated`) counts the messages. A run that does run
    out keeps its `discard`, `fileinto` and flags, drops its `redirect`,
    `reject` and `vacation`, and keeps the message if anything outward was
    dropped. A `set` that does not fit now stops the run instead of being
    ignored, and the numbered match variables no longer share the 64 KiB of
    the script's own variables. See docs/configuration.md.
  * JMAP: `VacationResponse/set` that switches the reply on, or changes one
    that is on, takes the `send` scope as well as `write`, like a Sieve script
    that replies; a key with `read` and `write` only gets `forbidden`.
    Switching it off needs only `write`.
  * JMAP: storing or activating a Sieve script that redirects, replies or
    rejects takes the `send` scope as well as `write`. `SieveScript/set` is one
    step under the account lock (`ifInState` is exact, the active script changes
    without a moment of no filter), and waiting for that lock no longer holds a
    runtime worker.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.12.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: Namecheap sent its API key in the query of the `getHosts` GET,
    and a failed connection or a timeout printed the whole URL, key
    included, in the error of `dns lockdown`, `dns provider test` and the
    DKIM daemon. `getHosts` is now a form POST like `setHosts`, and no DNS
    provider puts the request URL in an error.
  * Security: the payload of an IMAP APPEND refused over its size limit, and
    the bytes of an SMTP BDAT chunk refused for memory or sent outside a
    transaction, were run as commands. They are now read and discarded.
  * Security: rustls 0.23.46 fixes RUSTSEC-2026-0346 (secrets in the Debug
    output of client session values), 0347 (application data sent to the outer
    server after an ECH rejection), 0348 (Brotli certificate decompression that
    could allocate 1 GiB before authentication) and 0349 (a ServerHello that
    could downgrade later connections to classical key exchange). serde_json
    1.0.154 and opentelemetry-otlp 0.33.1 are updated too.
  * Security: with `encrypt_at_rest`, the outbound spool envelopes (sender,
    recipients, account, retry state) are sealed with the storage key and bound
    to their id. Envelopes written by 0.12.0 are still delivered and are sealed
    the next time they are read or edited. Spool files are created 0600 and
    directories 0700 whatever the umask, and the server audits and tightens
    them at start-up through no-follow descriptors; if it cannot, it refuses to
    start. `epistle queue`, `status` and `account remove --queue discard` open
    the spool with the configured key, and `status` reports spool encryption
    and loose permissions.
  * Security: inbound SMTP memory is bounded. `[server] message_memory_budget`
    is shared by every DATA and BDAT transaction, a client (an IPv4 address or
    an IPv6 /64) may hold at most a quarter of it, and a refused transaction
    gets `452 4.3.1`. Unset, it is a fifth of the memory of the `mail`
    container (its cgroup limit, else `mem_limit` in compose.yaml, else 1 GiB
    assumed), between `max_message_size` and 1 GiB; a value set by hand is
    kept, with a warning when it would take over 70% of the container.
    `max_connections_per_ip` (default 20, counted per IPv6 /64) is new: with
    rootless bridge networking every client arrives as one address, so all of
    them share the 20. `max_message_size` now also reaches the REST send route,
    which never received it in production.
  * Security: a client that sends a message too slowly no longer holds its
    memory. After `data_rate_grace` (120 s) the average rate of a DATA or BDAT
    transaction must stay at `min_data_rate` (10240 B/s) or the client gets
    `421 4.4.2 transfer too slow`. A session gets `421 4.7.0` after
    `junk_command_limit` (100) commands without progress (NOOP, RSET, unknown
    commands, refused MAIL or RCPT, and so on) and `421 4.4.2` after
    `max_session_time` (1800 s), never in the middle of a message body. `0`
    turns each off. All of them are set with `epistle limits set|unset|show`,
    as is `message_memory_budget`.
  * Fix: the per-account and per-domain submission rate limits never reached
    the server. One limiter per account now covers SMTP, REST and JMAP, each
    accepted recipient costs one unit, a charge is given back when the message
    is refused later or the send fails, and the refusal reads `sending rate
    limit exceeded; retry later` everywhere. An account in several limited
    domains gets the strictest limit. `epistle limits set submission-rate 0` is
    refused; use `unset`. A JMAP send without an envelope goes to the
    deduplicated To, Cc and Bcc only (RFC 8621), no longer to Resent-*
    addresses of an earlier copy.
  * Fix: inbound `rsa-sha256` DKIM signatures never verified. The verifier gave
    the crypto library the whole key record, a SubjectPublicKeyInfo as senders
    publish it, where it expects the bare RSA key. Mail from senders that sign
    only with RSA had no passing DKIM, so DMARC could align only through SPF.
    The verifier now unwraps the record and refuses malformed DER.
  * Upgrading from 0.12.0 needs no SQL migration. Without changing any setting,
    `epistle status` now fails on a mail name (MX target, SMTP host,
    autoconfig, autodiscover, mta-sts) behind the Cloudflare proxy, on a DNSSEC
    TLSA record that does not match the certificate in use, and on a duplicate
    SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT or DKIM record, and it warns when the
    container limits add up to over 85% of the host's memory. The default of
    `clamd_max_bytes` is 26279936 (it was 26214400). A configuration without
    `clamd_on_error` keeps `accept`, without `[attachments]` keeps `flag`, and
    the warm-up stays off; on a new install `epistle init` writes `defer` and
    `quarantine` and offers the warm-up. The `enable` and `disable` actions of
    `domain`, `alert`, `webhook` and `relay`, and `attachments policy
    set|unset`, write mail.toml and restart the `mail` container when the stack
    is installed, and otherwise say that the server must be restarted.
  * CLI: every command that prints data takes `--json` and then prints one
    JSON document on stdout, with notes and errors on stderr; the hidden flat
    forms (`accounts`, `account-add`, `app-password-create`, `api-keys` and
    the rest) take it too. The commands whose stdout is already the product
    (`serve`, `local`, `mta-sts-serve`, `export`, `backup`, `completions`,
    `report-abuse`, `mobileconfig`, `autoconfig`, `autodiscover`) and
    `stack logs` do not. docs/cli.md lists the result object.
  * CLI exit code 3 is new: the command is well formed and its target
    exists, but a rule of the server refuses the change. It replaces exit 2
    in four cases, so a script can tell "refused by policy or state" from "fix
    the command" (2) and "retry later" (1): a static account that only the
    configuration file may change (`account disable|remove|password|...`,
    `app-password create`), a limit that is reached (the masked-address
    limit, the other store limits), the `scim` scope on an API key bound to an
    account, and `account forward add` while `srs_secret` is not set.
    Unknown names, rejected input and a destructive action without `--yes`
    stay 2. A script that treated any non-zero code other than 1 as a usage
    error needs to read 3 as a refusal.
  * Mail classes: a credential has a class, `personal` (the default),
    `marketing` or `transactional`, set with `app-password create --class`,
    `api-key create --class` and `account set NAME --default-class`. The class
    is a floor: the `class` field of an API request or a JMAP submission may
    ask for a stricter one, never a looser one, and an `Epistle-Class` header
    changes nothing and is removed. `marketing` needs a one-click unsubscribe
    and never goes to a suppressed address. `transactional` is not copied to
    Sent, and is refused (SMTP `451 4.3.0`, API `503`, JMAP
    `serverUnavailable`) when its evidence cannot be written; the server takes
    none until it has verified the log once after a start.
  * Evidence of sending: acceptances, outcomes (delivered, bounced, expired,
    suppressed, abandoned, canceled) and key changes go into a hash-chained,
    append-only log with Merkle inclusion proofs, a signed checkpoint for each
    closed day and an RFC 3161 timestamp on it (Sectigo's public service by
    default); without a policy, `transactional` keeps metadata for 365 days.
    `epistle evidence
    verify|export|check|key|policy|timestamp|clock|acknowledge|abandon`.
    `evidence check` verifies a bundle offline and fails without `--key`: the
    reader brings the signing key (a fingerprint or a PEM file; an earlier key
    as `KEY@YYYY-MM-DD`), because a bundle cannot vouch for the key that signed
    it. `--require-delivered` makes it exit 1 unless every recipient was
    delivered. It is not certified email and does not prove that anyone read a
    message. See docs/evidence.md.
  * Antispam: `[attachments]` judges each attachment by its bytes, not its
    name: the real type against the name and the declared type, double and
    always-dangerous extensions (exe, scr, js, vbs, bat, lnk, iso and others,
    which `allow_extensions` cannot allow), zip entry names read without
    extracting, Office macros, encrypted and nested archives, SVG, and
    executables and zips hidden in images. What cannot be read to the end is
    never clean. `mode = off|flag|quarantine|reject` (`flag` adds a codes-only
    `X-Epistle-Attachment-Warning` header, which is stripped from inbound mail;
    `reject` is a 550 during DATA), plus `macros` and `encrypted_archives`.
    `epistle attachments policy show|set|unset` edits it and `epistle
    attachments check FILE.eml` judges a local message; with `--strict` it
    exits 3 on a quarantine or reject. See docs/attachments.md.
  * Antispam: `clamd_on_error = accept|defer`. With `defer`, a message clamd
    cannot scan gets `451 4.7.1` and never reaches a mailbox unscanned, and one
    clamd can never take (over `clamd_max_bytes`) is refused for good: `552
    5.3.4` on SMTP, `413 scan_too_large` on the API, `forbiddenToSend` on JMAP.
    `defer` requires `clamd_max_bytes` of at least `max_message_size` + 64 KiB,
    and the configuration is refused otherwise.
  * Queue: IP warm-up per receiver group (gmail, outlook, yahoo, icloud and
    other, read from the MX that answers). Each group has a daily cap that
    starts at 50 and is paced through the UTC day; recipients over it wait in
    the spool, which is not an attempt and does not count toward expiry. Each
    day is judged from the delivery log by reply codes: a good day raises the
    cap by half when at least half of it was used, a very bad one (over 10%
    deferrals, or a 5.7.x reply) lowers it, never under 50. `epistle warmup
    status|plan|start|enable|disable|pause|resume|set|reset`, all with
    `--json`; `status`, `queue list` and `queue destinations` show it. See
    docs/warmup.md.
  * Queue: delivery is concurrent and bounded. Every step of an attempt has a
    timeout and a whole attempt ends after 1 hour, but never once the final dot
    is sent. There is a global cap (8 by default), a cap per destination domain
    and per exchanger, and a sender takes at most 3/4 of the slots. A 421 or
    4.7.x reply puts the destination on hold for one backoff step, kept across
    restarts. `epistle limits set|unset|show
    queue-concurrency|queue-per-domain-concurrency|queue-backoff`, `epistle
    queue destinations` and `epistle queue release-destination
    DOMAIN|HOST|--all`. A broken spool entry no longer stops the scan.
  * Stack: `epistle stack resources [set SERVICE --memory SIZE --pids N]
    [--json]` lists and changes the container limits in compose.yaml (`mail` is
    refused less than five messages of `max_message_size` plus 256 MiB), and
    `epistle status` has a `resources` area. On a new install `epistle init`
    sizes the containers from the host memory (ClamAV is left out under 4 GiB);
    an existing install is not changed, and `status` suggests the commands.
  * DKIM: `[[dkim.domain]]` (`name`, `selector`, `key_file`, optional RSA
    selector and key) gives a domain its own signing key; `[dkim]` stays the
    default for the others. A domain that is not hosted, or listed twice, or
    with a missing or invalid key stops the start. `dns-records`, `verify-dns`,
    `status` and `dkim show` show each domain's own selector. Overridden
    domains are not rotated, and `dkim rotate --domain` refuses them. `epistle
    migrate mox` imports each domain's key as such an override.
  * DKIM and TLS: the automatic rotation now renews the RSA key too (2048 bits,
    selector `rsa<day>`) in the same journal entry as the ed25519 key: both
    switch together or neither does. With your own certificate (no ACME) the
    mail listeners check the files every 60 s and swap in a new pair only when
    the whole chain parses and the key matches; a bad pair is logged once and
    the old one stays. The TLSA record is republished through the DNS provider,
    or a warning gives the value to publish by hand. `epistle status` warns
    when such a certificate expires within 14 days and nothing renews it.
  * JMAP: `VacationResponse` (RFC 8621 section 8). JMAP and Sieve share one
    auto-reply path: an enabled JMAP vacation wins, otherwise a Sieve
    `vacation` answers. A reply goes out only if the sender is not a robot or a
    list (`List-*`, `Precedence: bulk|list|junk`, `Auto-Submitted`,
    `MAILER-DAEMON`), an address of the account is in To or Cc, the server's
    own SPF or DKIM check passed for the envelope sender's domain, and none
    went to that sender in the period (1 to 365 days). It goes to the envelope
    sender, carries `Auto-Submitted: auto-replied` and is DKIM signed.
  * IMAP: `THREAD=REFERENCES` (RFC 5256), the threading most clients use,
    computed without recursion so a hostile mailbox cannot overflow the stack,
    and `APPENDLIMIT=26214400` (RFC 7889): an APPEND over it gets `NO [TOOBIG]`
    and the connection stays open, where it used to close with BYE.
  * DNS: `epistle status` and `verify-dns` count the records with each tag at a
    name and fail when there is more than one SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS or TLS-RPT
    record, or the same DKIM selector twice (two SPF records make every
    receiver return `permerror`). Publishing now edits the first record of the
    purpose and deletes the other TXT records with that tag at that name, for
    every provider; TXT records of another purpose are left alone. `epistle
    status` also fails when a mail name is proxied by Cloudflare (orange
    cloud), and publishing sends `proxied: false` for them. A null MX no longer
    goes out with an empty host on GoDaddy, Spaceship, Porkbun and Namecheap,
    and Google Cloud DNS reads every page of a zone.
  * CLI: `epistle alias`, `domain`, `relay` and `alert` naming an unknown
    target exit 2, as other unknown names do, where they exited 1. `account
    add`, `remove`, `password`, `enable` and `disable` wait up to 5 s until the
    running server has loaded the change, so a script that adds an account and
    logs in at once no longer trips the brute-force ban; on timeout they note
    it and exit 0. `account show` shows forwards, catch-all and two-factor for
    accounts defined in mail.toml too, `account add` validates the names before
    asking for the password, and creating or removing an app password keeps
    what another process wrote meanwhile.
  * CLI: `epistle domain disable|enable` switches a hosted domain off and on
    without removing it. The new `disabled_domains` setting lists the
    switched-off domains; mail for one is rejected like mail for an unknown
    user and nobody may send as an address in it. Accounts, DNS records and
    DKIM keys stay. `domain list` and `domain show` report `enabled`. A
    disabled domain gets no autoconfig/autodiscover document, `GET
    /api/v1/domains` names it under `disabled`, `masked add` refuses it and
    `account add`/`alias add` warn about it. `domain disable` says how many
    queued messages still have a sender in the domain (they are delivered), and
    the docs state that a login with an address in the disabled domain fails.
  * CLI: `epistle domain set` writes the storage quota and the submission
    rate of one domain (the overrides `limits set --domain` already wrote),
    and `domain show` prints them.
  * CLI: `epistle alert show|enable|disable`. A rule switched off with the new
    `enabled = false` key stays in the file but takes no samples and never
    fires; `alert list` marks it and its JSON row has `enabled`.
  * CLI: `epistle webhook enable|disable`. `enabled = false` in `[webhook]`
    keeps the URL and the secret but posts no event, alerts included; `webhook
    test` still posts. `webhook show` prints `enabled`. `webhook disable` lists
    the events and alerts that go quiet, needs `--yes` while an alert posts to
    the webhook, and `config-check` warns about such an alert.
  * CLI: `epistle relay enable|disable`. A relay rule with `enabled = false`
    stays in the file but is never selected, so its mail goes by the next
    matching rule or direct to the MX. Only a relay rule can be switched off;
    the loader refuses `enabled = false` on a `fail` or `direct` rule.
    `relay disable` says where the mail will go instead (the next matching
    rule, or directly to the MX from this server's own address) and needs
    `--yes` off a terminal.
  * Fix: `epistle queue remove` panicked with "there is no reactor running"
    when a webhook was configured (the command line had no runtime to post the
    `canceled` event on); it now posts it before exiting.
  * CLI: `epistle suppression show|export|import`, and `suppression add
    --reason`. `export` writes the list as CSV, `import` loads such a file or
    a plain list of addresses (16 MiB and 100000 entries at most, checked whole
    before anything is written). `suppression add` no longer says it
    suppressed an address whose marker it could not write, and `suppression
    list` escapes control characters in what it prints.
  * CLI: `epistle app-password rotate|rename`. `rotate` replaces the secret
    and prints the new one once, keeping the label, expiry, address range and
    `save-sent` setting; the old secret stops working at once. `rename` keeps
    the secret. App password labels are now 1 to 64 characters without
    control characters when created or renamed, and `app-password list`
    escapes control characters.
  * CLI: `epistle delivery show ID` prints every record of one message and
    `delivery export` writes the filtered records as CSV (or JSON), at most
    100000. `delivery list` takes `--until`, and `--status` as another name
    of `--outcome`. The delivery log is now read a line at a time (a line over
    1 MiB is skipped), days outside `--since`/`--until` are not opened, and
    `delivery list --limit` stops at 100000 like `export`.
  * CLI: `epistle db status` prints the PostgreSQL version, size, tables and
    connections from one read-only query in the stack's database container,
    and gives up after 15 seconds, stopping what the query started with it.
  * DNS providers: a listing keeps every record under its own type. NS, HTTPS,
    SVCB and CAA were read as TXT by some providers and dropped by others; a
    type epistle has no use for (NAPTR, the SOA, Namecheap's URL redirects,
    Bunny's redirect and script records) is kept under its type name. A type
    name that is not letters, digits and hyphens (at most 32) shows as UNKNOWN.
    OVH and Porkbun no longer cut the closing quote off a CAA or HTTPS value.
  * DNS providers: every call has a 10 second connect and a 30 second total
    timeout, an operation (a listing with all its pages, a publish with its
    reads) a 120 second deadline, redirects are not followed, and a response
    body past 16 MiB (256 KiB for Namecheap) is refused as it arrives.
    Before, twelve providers waited for ever and read any body whole; RFC 2136
    now has its own connect and exchange timeouts and the same deadline. The
    bodies of one operation share a 64 MiB budget. A listing past 1000 pages
    or 100000 records is an error for every provider; one whose pages do not
    add up to the total it gives is an error (Spaceship, Cloudflare, DNSimple,
    DigitalOcean); a full page that does not say whether more follow is an
    error (Cloudflare, DNSimple, Spaceship); and deSEC lists zones of more
    than 500 rrsets page by page. A listing keeps the preference of an MX and
    the flags and tag of a CAA, and Spaceship sends and reads the MX field as
    `preference`; those field layouts come from the providers' API references
    and are not checked against the real services.
  * DNS: `epistle dns lockdown [DOMAIN] [--dry-run] [--yes] [--json] [--undo]
    [--dmarc reject]` stops anyone from sending mail as the domain from names
    that do not send mail. It reads the zone through the DNS provider and plans
    a null SPF (`v=spf1 -all`) and a null MX (`0 .`) on every name with an
    address that does not send mail (skipping the names epistle serves and
    those with their own MX, SPF or CNAME), a wildcard null SPF when the zone
    has no wildcard, and DMARC one step per run (none, quarantine, reject;
    `--dmarc reject` jumps to reject). Nothing is written without confirmation.
    A journal lets `--undo` remove only the records that still match what was
    written; a record the operator changed since is left alone.
  * `epistle dns lockdown` leaves delegations alone: a name below the zone
    apex with NS records, and every name under it, gets no record and is
    listed once as delegated to other nameservers. A domain that is itself
    delegated gets no DMARC rewrite and no wildcard. The plan is built in one
    pass over the zone, a name that cannot be a DNS name is reported cut down,
    and control characters from the zone are escaped in the output. A name
    that is not letters, digits, hyphens and underscores is listed as left
    alone and never written to, a wildcard hidden by a delegation does not
    block the domain's wildcard, and `--undo` leaves a record in a name
    delegated since. See docs/dns.md.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:27:09 +0000

epistle (0.12.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Migration: `epistle migrate imap` copies a remote account from any IMAP
    server (resumable, keeps folders, flags and dates), and `epistle migrate
    mox` / `epistle migrate stalwart` import domains, accounts, aliases and
    DKIM keys with their selectors, so DNS does not change. See
    docs/migration.md.
  * Subfolders: mailbox names may contain `/` (Work/Project) in IMAP, JMAP
    (parentId), Sieve fileinto and Maildir export/import. CREATE makes the
    missing parents, RENAME moves children, DELETE of a parent with children
    answers HASCHILDREN. No data migration.
  * Sent copies: mail an account sends through SMTP submission, the API or
    JMAP is saved in its Sent folder by default, without duplicating the copy
    a client appends itself. `epistle account save-sent`, `app-password
    create --save-sent` and `"save_sent": false` on the API turn it off.
  * Delivery log: one metadata-only line per recipient outcome under
    data_dir/deliverylog, kept 30 days ([storage] delivery_log_days, 0 turns
    it off), read with `epistle delivery list` or GET /api/v1/deliveries.
    New webhooks delivered, delayed, suppressed and canceled, and an `extra`
    correlation map on /api/v1/send.
  * IMAP, checked with Dovecot's imaptest in CI (69 of 73 scripts pass; the
    rest test \Recent, which IMAP4rev2 removed): other sessions' expunges
    and flag changes are announced in order, STORE no longer undoes another
    session's change, SEARCH supports BODY, SENT*, every HEADER field and
    parsed addresses, CONDSTORE is remembered, SORT and THREAD follow RFC
    5256, APPEND keeps its date and zone, ENVELOPE, BINARY and message/rfc822
    parts are complete, UIDVALIDITY is never reused, NOTIFY SET STATUS
    works, LIST understands wildcards and the reference, and the tail of a
    rejected literal is no longer read as a command.
  * Security: IMAP lines are bounded at 32 KiB with linear scanning, search
    nesting at 64 levels, the base subject is computed in linear time, and
    the migration client bounds memory by the bytes it receives.
  * CLI: run as root, `epistle` hands the files an operator names
    (password files, a Mox config, an import or export Maildir) to the
    service account through a private staging copy, and moves outputs
    back owned by root; `epistle delivery list` is the delivery log
    reader.


 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 02:14:22 +0000

epistle (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: API and JMAP push subscriptions belong to their creator; a
    WebSocket or event-source session closes once its credential is revoked
    or its account disabled; HTTP routes refuse a disabled account's key;
    JMAP reads need the read scope; TLS-RPT reports are posted only to public
    addresses; DMARC reports go only to plain addresses the policy domain
    authorizes; SCRAM verifiers use 600000 iterations and TOTP secrets are
    encrypted at rest; account-bound keys stay within their account.
  * Mail protocols: no second banner inside TLS after STARTTLS (mail
    between two epistle servers failed), commands pipelined behind STARTTLS
    are dropped, DMARC evaluates the From a reader sees, submissions with a
    From the account does not own are refused, DKIM signs for the From
    domain, a rotated DKIM key is used only once DNS shows it, DANE applies
    only behind a DNSSEC-secure MX and never falls back to cleartext on a
    bogus TLSA, every listener survives a failed accept and bounds TLS
    handshakes and HTTP request heads.
  * Sending: the API and JMAP go through the same path as SMTP submission
    (DKIM signed, local recipients delivered directly) and build complete
    messages (text and HTML, attachments, inline parts, threading and list
    headers); Bcc never leaves the server; mail the server's own accounts
    send is scanned for viruses; every stored copy gets Return-Path and
    Delivered-To, and a forwarding loop is bounced; TLS-Required: No is
    honoured for our own senders.
  * MTA-STS: a policy fetch failure is reported in TLS-RPT and a new
    policy id is picked up before max_age.
  * IMAP: new keywords are announced in FLAGS before the FETCH that uses
    them, new messages are announced after APPEND and on NOOP, and a
    LITERAL+ APPEND gets no stray continuation.
  * DNS providers: TXT values reach each of the 13 providers in the form
    its API expects, an update replaces only the record of the same
    purpose, and Namecheap uses the request its API documents (it needs
    `[dns] client_ip` now).
  * DKIM rotation is journalled: a crash at any step completes or rolls
    back on the next start, and `epistle dkim cleanup` removes orphans.
  * JMAP: incremental sync with /changes and /queryChanges from a per-account
    change journal, push over an event source, and the limits the Session
    advertises are enforced.
  * CLI: every resource answers to epistle <resource> <action> with --json
    and consistent exit codes; destructive commands ask first (--yes skips),
    passwords are read without echo, shell completions and man pages ship
    in the package; the account stores lock across CLI and server.
  * Delivery: greylisting persists in PostgreSQL, DMARC reports are signed
    and well formed, ACME supports TLS-ALPN-01 and DNS-01, TLS-RPT reports
    are sent, autoconfig is served on 443, Sieve gains the mailbox
    extension, CalDAV/CardDAV support sync-collection and filters.
  * Mail addresses, aliases and domains are stored in lowercase whoever
    writes them; a refused address names the rule it breaks; accounts get
    a free-text display name used by JMAP, SCIM and the send API.
  * Outbound delivery decides per recipient: one refused address no longer
    stops the others, no recipient is sent twice, and the remote reply is
    classified by its enhanced status code (mailbox full is retried, policy
    rejections are bounced but never suppress the address, a null MX fails
    at once). Retry timing is configurable and spread with jitter.
  * Inbound SMTP enforces the mailbox quota with 452 4.2.2, answers RCPT
    before MAIL with 503, and every error reply carries an enhanced code.
  * epistle status checks clamd from the mail container when the stack
    holds its socket.
  * The service account gets a subordinate id range no other account holds;
    an upgrade moves a range 0.10.1 shared with the first user.
  * Upgrades: stack update renders the compose file again, pins images by
    digest, dumps the database before restarting, checks health after it,
    and `epistle stack rollback` goes back to the previous binary;
    `epistle db upgrade` moves PostgreSQL to a new major; on SIGTERM the
    server drains open SMTP and IMAP sessions.
  * Stack: the server's PostgreSQL role is no longer a superuser, keys live
    outside data_dir, containers drop every capability they do not need,
    run read-only and have memory and process limits.
  * `epistle backup` works on servers that have sent mail (paths over 100
    bytes failed since 0.10.0).
  * The CLI marks states with symbols and colour on a terminal, shows
    progress on long commands, offers a list when an account, alias or
    domain is missing, and `epistle init` creates the first account with
    postmaster@ and abuse@.
  * PostgreSQL 18 is the minimum supported server.

