epistle (0.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * epistle runs as a container stack. `sudo epistle init` writes the
    configuration and a compose file for podup, and `sudo epistle stack
    up` brings up mail (on pasta, so the real client address reaches
    the SPF and rate-limit checks), PostgreSQL and ClamAV, the last two
    without any network and reached over Unix sockets. Both commands
    run as the glyndor-epistle account even when started with sudo. The
    stack comes back after a reboot, and apt upgrades refresh the
    server image. podup (>= 5.10.13) is now a dependency.
  * A public hostname gets a certificate from Let's Encrypt by default,
    and IMAPS (993) and submissions (465) are listened on next to the
    STARTTLS ports.
  * Mail data defaults to /var/lib/glyndor/epistle/data, outside the
    service account's home, which holds Podman's storage and the
    account's systemd units.
  * DNS lookups go over TCP. Behind the systemd-resolved stub, the
    UDP replies to DNSSEC queries arrived cut in the middle of a
    record, and every SPF, MX and TLSA lookup failed.
  * IMAP works with ordinary mail clients. FETCH returns ENVELOPE,
    BODYSTRUCTURE, body sections and partial ranges, read from the real
    MIME tree; the server advertises IMAP4rev1 next to IMAP4rev2; new
    accounts get Sent, Drafts, Trash and Archive. A failed SELECT
    deselects, CLOSE expunges, saved searches follow messages, `*` in a
    UID set is the highest UID, and IDLE reports another session's
    expunges.
  * JMAP Email objects carry the real MIME structure, body values and
    downloadable attachments instead of one text/plain part.
  * Accounts and app passwords changed with the CLI take effect within
    a few seconds, without a restart.
  * `epistle backup` dumps the stack's database inside its container
    and fails when the dump can't be taken, instead of writing an
    archive without it. `epistle restore` is new.
  * Hardening in the protocol readers: rejected literals are discarded
    instead of being read as commands, request bodies, JMAP
    back-references, Sieve nesting and four parsers of untrusted input
    are bounded, WebDAV refuses paths through symlinks, and SCRAM goes
    through the shared ban table.
  * DNS providers: porkbun, digitalocean, rfc2136 and the new GoDaddy
    provider can be selected, an unknown provider name is refused, and
    every provider follows one rule for replacing and deleting TXT
    records.
  * Report ingest, antispam and conformance fixes in POP3, ManageSieve,
    WebDAV and JMAP.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Uploaded blobs can live somewhere other than the local disk. A
    BlobBackend trait with two implementations: the filesystem one is
    what was there before, moved behind the trait and unchanged, and an
    S3 one that speaks to any S3-compatible endpoint. The secret comes
    from an environment variable or a file, never from the TOML. SigV4
    is signed directly rather than pulling in the AWS SDK for four HTTP
    verbs, and the signature is checked against the vector AWS
    publishes, not against this implementation. Absent or backend = "fs"
    is the default and keeps the previous behaviour: an existing config
    parses unchanged and never opens a socket.
  * Tenants have limits, not just domains. A [[tenant]] block accepts
    quota_bytes, max_accounts, max_domains and
    submission_rate_limit_per_min, all aggregated across the tenant's
    domains. A cap that is reached answers 409 rather than 429: waiting
    does not lift it. A quota below the sum of the domains' own quotas
    is rejected on load, because it could never be reached.
  * Submission rate limits can differ per domain.
    domain_submission_limits maps a domain to messages per minute, and
    an account picks up the entry for its own domain; without one the
    server-wide default applies, and without that there is no limit. The
    domain comes from the account's own address rather than from the
    first one configured.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Uploaded JMAP blobs are stored two levels deep under
    blobs/<ab>/<cd>/<id> instead of in one flat directory. The reclaim
    sweep and the quota usage count both walk that directory on a timer,
    so flat they cost the whole history of the install rather than what
    is there now, and most filesystems slow down badly past a few tens
    of thousands of entries in one place. The shard comes from the END
    of the blob id: ids are UUIDv7, whose leading characters are a
    timestamp, so sharding on them would file everything written in the
    same era into one bucket. There is no migration and nothing to
    configure: a blob written by an older version stays where it is and
    is still served, still counted against quota, and still reclaimed.
  * The master at-rest key and the base64 text it was read from are
    zeroized after loading, so the buffers are overwritten on drop
    rather than left in freed memory, a core dump, or swap. Best effort,
    not a guarantee, and the doc comment says which gaps remain: the
    live key keeps its own copy for the life of the process.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * IMAP COMPRESS=DEFLATE (RFC 4978). A client that asks gets a deflate
    stream in both directions for the rest of the connection; one that
    does not is unaffected, and there is nothing to configure. The
    compression context is kept for the whole connection rather than
    reset per message, which is where the saving comes from: IMAP
    repeats the same command names, flag names and header keys
    endlessly. The tagged OK for the command itself travels
    uncompressed, as the RFC requires, and a second COMPRESS answers
    NO [COMPRESSIONACTIVE] rather than restarting the context underneath
    a client that is already decoding.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Eight new DNS providers: DigitalOcean, RFC 2136 dynamic update over
    TSIG, Google Cloud DNS, Porkbun, Bunny.net, DNSimple, Spaceship and
    OVH. With Cloudflare, deSEC, Route 53 and Namecheap that is twelve.
  * The records to go with them. Epistle already published SPF, DKIM,
    DMARC and MTA-STS; it now also publishes TLSRPT, the SRV discovery
    set (_submissions, _imaps, _pop3s, _jmap, _sieve, and _caldavs and
    _carddavs when WebDAV is on), the autoconfig, autodiscover and
    mta-sts CNAMEs, and CAA. MX had always been emitted and every
    provider rejected it as unsupported; all twelve accept it now.
  * CAA is derived from the directory_url in the acme section, never
    hardcoded. An unrecognised directory publishes no CAA rather than a
    guess, because a CAA naming the wrong CA does not degrade issuance,
    it blocks it, renewals included.
  * Metric alerts, delivered by webhook or email.
  * SCIM 2.0 provisioning under /scim/v2, on its own scope so an
    identity-provider integration can be held tighter than the
    operator's own access.
  * LLM-assisted screening for the uncertain band of the spam filter.
    This is the first decision in epistle that reads the Bayesian score
    rather than only training it.
  * Deleted mail can be kept and restored. Set
    [storage] deleted_retention_days above zero and an expunge moves the
    message into the account archive instead of deleting it, with an
    hourly sweep, a CLI and an API. Zero is the default and keeps the
    previous behaviour exactly, down to not creating the directory.
  * Masked email addresses: throwaway addresses that deliver to an
    account without revealing it. Disabling one makes it reject exactly
    the way an address that never existed does.
  * An API key may list domains and is then held to those domains, and
    to the accounts that live entirely inside them. A key without the
    field reaches everything, which is what every key did before, so an
    existing api_keys.toml survives the upgrade without edits.
  * IMAP CHILDREN, WITHIN and SEARCHRES.
  * Account names coming from a SQL or LDAP directory are checked before
    they are used. The name doubles as a directory name under the data
    directory, and only config, API and SCIM accounts were being
    validated, so a directory row holding a path traversal reached the
    filesystem through the authenticated session. Rows that cannot be
    named safely are dropped and logged rather than failing the whole
    refresh.
  * SCRAM salts are drawn already filled instead of overwriting a zeroed
    buffer, so there is no window where a deterministic salt sits in the
    buffer about to be used.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * JMAP EmailSubmission/set now checks address ownership. A request that
    submits from an address the named account does not own is rejected.
    This is a behaviour change, not a regression - integrations that
    submitted from a sender the account did not own stop working, and
    that is the correct answer.
  * API keys carry scopes. A key without the field keeps the full access
    it had before, so an existing api_keys.toml survives the upgrade
    without edits - a warning is logged once per key the first time it
    is loaded. New keys require scopes.
  * JMAP blob downloads are now gated to the owning account. On startup
    the owner of every existing blob that some message references is
    backfilled, so the change applies to data already on disk. A blob
    uploaded but never referenced by a message stops being served.
  * Privilege changes are now logged. Adding or removing TOTP, changing
    the password, and deleting an account each record an event with the
    account and the client IP. The TOTP secret, the password and its
    hash are never written to the log.
  * Header values controlled by third parties are sanitised at JMAP
    message construction time and in the abuse reports.
  * The README and the installer now point to the apt archive at
    apt.glyndor.net/install/epistle (#712).

